To this day, I can still picture the line of hungry tourists wrapped up around the corner from this building, coming out of the downtown Vancouver subway platforms in the spring of 1996. The weather may have been a few degrees below zero or a bit below a full-blown polar vortex (it can happen that way once in Canada, especially on the west coast), but the sheer excitement and vibrancy of the city, combined with the fresh smells of the street vendors hawking everything from dried mushrooms to grilled meats to local craft products at their kiosks were enough to convince most of us to give this place a second look.
For most of my Canadian travels prior to this, I either stuck to my base of Vancouver every year, or chose British Columbia as a regional "destination city" of sorts because of the proximity to my other favourite trips: Vancouver and its famous Stanley Park (see my posts from Vancouver and Victoria); Abbotsford, the largest agricultural city outside of BC's main cities;

, NJ
